re you  decide if anyone's gonna take your life  it's gonna be you can you talk a little  bit about shooting that particular scene  oh yeah sure  hello everybody so yeah we did 17 days  all of my work was completed within five  days right  yeah it's called preparation that scene  in particular for me was sort of having  to explore the idea of the value of the  true value of life because you know he's  sitting there as someone is literally  saying this is how I'm going to take  your life these are your last precious  moments and these it calls to mind the  idea of what we take for granted in so  many ways that we don't even realize you  know so for for me I wanted to explore  what that was through Anthony's eyes  through his emotions and as you you know  saw he's not saying anything  he's has to process this whole idea of  his own personal value at least that was  the process for me and I thought it was  really a beautiful scene beautifully  executed one of my favorite scenes that  I've ever been able to perform just  because of how much it meant and the  challenge of trying to say so much  without having to say anything at all  and I thought it was a really sort of  smartly executed because it takes us all  a minute to just sit and be quiet and  think about what is the real value here  in terms of the daily lives we lead what  is the real value of the freedom that we  have that we may not you know fully  engage in a fully take advantage of you  know so I thought it was wonderful and  then of course in order for me to really  pull out that sort of performance I had  to rely on these two queens right here I  you know Chenoa I trusted you fully you  know say look you got it we're gonna get  it all right if you good we good and  then also pulling that emotion that for  me was you know sort of playing  ping-pong back and forth with with  Alfred just being able to work off her  how she was given that energy what she  was giving to me in that moment is what  I had to use to find those moments of  levity and then find those deeper  moments so it really was a team effort  that entire scene and I thank y'all for  that Richard what's interesting to me  about this film is that this is of  course a film about capital punishment  but it's also a film about the choices  you make in your life as you get down a  certain Road we think about can I do  this anymore which we saw play out in  both of your characters and can you just  I mean it's interesting because as an  actor it's one of the few professions  that hopefully it doesn't get so taxing  that you could keep doing it forever but  you could see as being this lawyer and  doing the work that he would do that it  would hit a point where it's a little  bit too much that scene about the baby  lawyer and the snapchat can can you talk  about working through the baby lawyer in  the snapchat scene man you know to see  can you just talk about that you know  playing the emotions of trying to figure  out what you're doing in that later  chapter of your life yeah I can try I  the thing that was interesting about  this character was as I think about it  now is is his own kind of death you know  I mean this movies really about the  collateral damage and the real damage of  the choice of killing someone and and  and this character I've been fascinated  with people who devote their lives in  this case to a to to work where if you  lose the the guy dies if you don't win  if you play a game of chess and if you  lose someone dies that's a tough that's  a tough life and I looked at him like he  was already kind of dying from from just  doing this and I remember that scene we  shot at the bar and I  who can barely look at Alfre and I  noticed that that she probably needed  some kind of connection or at least  that's what I felt and I just I just  couldn't you know I mean it was it was a  disdain for her because she represented  my life you know what I mean  so that's that's what was kind of  kicking around and felt he felt a little  bit like emotionally you know the scenes  with this lovely gentleman is amazing  actor you know when he when he had hope  he's expressed such great hope for my  ability to do something and it was it  just killed me it just killed me every  time I felt the hope you know it  surprised me and killed me what is  interesting because I think in a simpler  version of this story your character  would be the really good guy and  Bernardine would be the bad guy but I  don't think that anybody could walk away  from this feeling like Bernardin can you  talk a little bit Alfre about how you  came to understood why mean as you  mentioned that she wasn't necessarily  beating up bunnies as a kid so why do  you think she did this and did it make  you feel at all differently about the  people who do this work well that was  one of the reasons one of the great  benefits of acting is that you the  requirement is that you never stop  learning every time you you approach  them work you've got to learn about  somebody else's life everything their  history their the Anthropology of their  communities all of that because your job  is to leave yourself behind you the way  you speak though your gait your opinions  the way you see the world and just find  that person find the human being and  tell the story looking out from their  eyes and so that's that's beautiful and  so everything I needed was meeting those  women because I had no idea what that's  like I'm on the petition  side don't put Tookie Williams to death  Stanley Tookie Williams that we executed  here in California so but then that's  not my job as the artists or as the  actor so I see I believe them it's like  we need warriors we need them I'm a  pacifist but I understand that but I  know that I can't put myself in that in  that position because I am too emotional  that's why we do this work and I admired  their ability to stay balanced and to  say no sorry your mom's dead but you  can't go because if you